From c8812112bb3de42965f18fe6201f931d6c4201f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:26:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Navigation: the turn-on bias stays for the flight, in the
 truth and the filter
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The owner's decision of 2026-10-17 ("ก.", option a): each gyro and
accelerometer bias is a turn-on constant drawn at power-up plus an in-run
first-order Gauss-Markov process on top of it, as sensors.ts and
PHYSICS.md section 2h describe (Groves, 2nd ed., ch. 4 and section 14.2).
Until now both were one Gauss-Markov process started at their combined
size, so the turn-on part faded with the 300 s (a MEMS gyro's 30 deg/h was
5 deg/h after a quarter of an hour).

Truth: per axis b_on ~ N(0, turnOn^2), constant; b_run starts at
N(0, instab^2), decays with e^(-dt/tau) and is driven by
instab*sqrt(1 - e^(-2dt/tau)). The IMU output carries b_on + b_run. The
in-run parts are drawn after the alignment's errors, so a seed's turn-on
biases are the normals that seed gave the whole bias before.

Filter: 27 errors instead of 21. The turn-on biases are random constants
(F = 0, Q = 0, P0 = turnOn^2), the in-run biases Gauss-Markov
(F = -1/tau, Q = 2 instab^2/tau, P0 = instab^2); both enter phi-dot and
v-dot through -C alike; the measurements are unchanged. Between fixes the
turn-on estimates are held and the in-run estimates relax with e^(-dt/tau).
The covariance is propagated over Phi's non-zero entries only (at most 99
of 729) and its upper triangle mirrored, so the 27-state step is cheaper
than the dense 21-state one was: 20 against 31 and 25 against 42 us per
navigation step in two benchmarks (medians of 6 interleaved runs each,
tactical with GNSS and star tracker at 100 Hz, under shared load).

NavigationRecord's gyroBias, gyroBiasEstimate, accelBias and
accelBiasEstimate are the totals (turn-on plus in-run), so the telemetry
and the inspector's bias rows keep their meaning; the CSV has no bias
columns. A new getter, turnOnBiases, gives the turn-on parts with their
sigma.

Tests (tests/navigation.test.ts):
- the existing outage test (tactical, 100 s of fixes, 600 s outage,
  20 seeds) still holds: mean (error/sigma)^2 0.89 per axis (< 2);
- new: at 1500 s the true biases' RMS over 200 MEMS seeds is
  sqrt(turnOn^2 + instab^2): ratio 1.04 (gyro) and 0.97 (accel), within
  [0.9, 1.15]; the in-run part alone would be 0.16 and 0.04;
- new: a 1500 s unaided coast (tactical, 200 seeds) is consistent: the
  mean of (error/sigma)^2 per axis is 0.78-1.20 for position, velocity and
  attitude, inside chi2(200)/200's 99.9 % band [0.70, 1.36];
- new: with GNSS and the star tracker for 2000 s in orbit (tactical,
  20 seeds), the turn-on estimates converge to the constant parts:
  RMS error 0.15 deg/h of 1 and 20 ug of 500, (error/sigma)^2 0.8 and 0.7.

The MCP tool description and the inspector's note (en, ru, th) say 27
states.

